well let's have a look now at the  strategic situation on the ground in  ukraine i'm joined by air marshal edward  stringer good afternoon ed thanks for  being here um so just explain to us what  exactly is going on in the south  does ukraine have the strategic ability  to do what they're hoping to do  well zelinski's been quite clear that  he's not going to talk about the  tactical aims of this military operation  but they have been at the strategic  level signaling now for weeks that they  are going to  take the initiative with and move on to  the offensive in the southern region of  ukraine  so what we're seeing now in this area  you can see to the north and west of the  dunipa river which the russians had  taken we can see the ukrainian forces  exerting a very comprehensive push  across the whole territory  we've seen them accelerate their use of  precision missiles and artillery on the  remaining one or two significant lines  of communication to further choke off  any reinforcements or resupply to that  force and that is to make  the russian holding of that land pretty  untenable and clearly one way or another  the ukrainians are now hoping to force  the russians back across the dunepa  river because they no longer have the  combat power to hold that territory  and the south is a strategically  important area  anyway  how important do you think it is that  ukraine takes the initiative now this  moment particularly before winter  approaches  yes that's a very that's a very good  question uh it's strategically important  because of those main ports  strategically important to ukraine but  also strategically important to russia  because crimea has its only warm water  port for the black sea fleet in  sevastopol and also has some very major  airfields military bases and  and logistic  hubs  but what zolensky is trying to do is to  make sure that he can demonstrate he can  seize the initiative and he's not just  reacting to  a war that's being dictated by russia he  wants to show that he can go on on the  offensive and therefore demonstrate  principally to the west  that ukraine is capable of prevailing  over russia and therefore  allow western political leaders to  continue to support him when times get  tight with the economic crisis with the  price of gas throughout the autumn into  winter and maybe into next year  also if they do succeed in pushing the  russian forces  out of the west bank then actually  ukrainian forces are now very much  closer and within range of crimea which  is going to pose a serious strategic  military problem for russia so in both  the military and political spheres this  would mean a major success for zielinski  would allow him to retain that political  support  and as we've been talking a lot about mr  gorbachev today following his passing  one of president putin's aims has been  to rebuild the soviet union that mr  gorbachev lost  do you think there's any chance he'll  succeed  well putin is no gorbachev  and actually gorbachev genuinely  believed in the union of soviet  socialist republics and perestroika and  glasnost attempts to try and get it to  work properly in a way perhaps that the  chinese arguably have uh have been able  to do  but of course that led to the breakup of  that soviet union and to his great  credit gorbachev did not use violence or  force to keep the union together even  though the hard line has pressed him to  do so  putin is one of those hardliners he has  shown he is very willing to use force to  subjugate territories and i don't think  he's trying to rebuild the soviet union  he is much more cyrus he's trying to  rebuild the russian empire with a series  of scared nations having to do his  bidding as he's only managed to take  less than 20 of ukraine at the moment  after six months i don't think he has  much power left to do anything else and  therefore yes i think he is set to fail  in that ambition  hey marshall ed stringer thanks very  much for that  thank you
 

